WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.719
Memory safety by design in Rust is a
layered engineering practice.

2
00:00:03.799 --> 00:00:06.672
Use ownership and borrowing to make
responsibility clear.

3
00:00:06.752 --> 00:00:10.776
Let lifetimes, references, and the borrow
checker expose design questions early.

4
00:00:10.856 --> 00:00:14.707
Model valid states with types so invalid
combinations are harder to express and

5
00:00:14.787 --> 00:00:17.086
downstream code receives clearer
guarantees.

6
00:00:17.166 --> 00:00:19.177
Build safe APIs that protect invariants.

7
00:00:19.257 --> 00:00:23.281
Keep internal representation private when
callers should not modify it directly.

8
00:00:23.361 --> 00:00:26.752
Define what public interfaces guarantee,
what callers must provide,

9
00:00:26.832 --> 00:00:29.246
and which states are impossible through
safe use.

10
00:00:29.326 --> 00:00:31.797
Prefer checked access, clear parsing
boundaries,

11
00:00:31.877 --> 00:00:35.901
and explicit rejection behavior when
working with collections, strings, slices,

12
00:00:35.981 --> 00:00:36.958
and external input.

13
00:00:37.038 --> 00:00:40.946
Treat unsafe code, FFI, and concurrency as
small, documented trust boundaries.

14
00:00:41.026 --> 00:00:43.670
Unsafe code should be isolated, justified,
reviewed,

15
00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:45.762
and wrapped in safe APIs where practical.

16
00:00:45.842 --> 00:00:48.888
FFI should document ownership, allocation,
thread, pointer,

17
00:00:48.968 --> 00:00:50.635
and representation assumptions.

18
00:00:50.715 --> 00:00:54.336
Concurrent and async designs need explicit
ownership, synchronization,

19
00:00:54.416 --> 00:00:56.945
cancellation, backpressure, and resource
limits.

20
00:00:57.025 --> 00:01:00.014
Finally, support compiler checks with
tests, tools, review,

21
00:01:00.094 --> 00:01:01.531
and continuous improvement.

22
00:01:01.611 --> 00:01:03.507
Test boundary cases and unusual input.

23
00:01:03.587 --> 00:01:05.254
Use tooling where it fits the risk.

24
00:01:05.334 --> 00:01:08.898
Review assumptions around unsafe,
indexing, panics, public contracts,

25
00:01:08.978 --> 00:01:10.587
shared state, and dependencies.

26
00:01:10.667 --> 00:01:12.219
Rust gives strong safety tools;

27
00:01:12.299 --> 00:01:15.460
disciplined design turns those tools into
dependable software.
